  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nFocus in the long-term will continue to be on the dangerous heat\nbuilding into the area, the growing drought conditions, and the\nincreasing fire weather concerns. Heat headlines will likely be \nneeded for at least portions of the forecast area almost each day \nthrough the long-term period.\n\nBroad upper-level ridging will overspread much of the central and\nsouthern CONUS through next week and into next weekend. Mesoscale\nboundaries and upper-level troughs, that could potentially bring\ncooler temperatures and meaningful rainfall, will stay well away\nfrom the forecast area for the foreseeable future. As southerly \nwinds continue to advect low-level moisture into the region over \nthe next several days, dewpoints will continue to rise each day.\nGuidance even suggests there could be enough low-level moisture \nfor brief, diurnally-driven showers and storms, especially in the\nhigh terrain areas of the forecast area, beginning mid to late \nnext week. Otherwise, rainfall looks unlikely until possibly the \nend of the workweek and into next weekend when the upper-level\nridge shifts west of the area again. But at this time, forecast \nconfidence is very low with regards to the next best chance of \nrain.\n\nMejia",
